    {
      "log_path": "stages/optimize/work/.kernelgen/claude-runtime.log",
      "log_sha256": "40bce9f247ac16f6f1809017556094cb5c8877bac37a837013f14e00d6c63bbe",
      "character_offset": 172405,
      "tool_use_id": "call_00_shwNPpxqK2d4STMsDUah3834",
      "arguments": {
        "subagent_type": "kernel-profile-analyzer",
        "description": "Profile log10_ round 1",
        "prompt": "Profile measured round_num=1 for the log10_ operator (kernel path tmp/main.py in this workspace). Use the native profile tools with round_num=1.\n\nKey measured facts from the authoritative eval (do not recompute, use as context):\n- geo_mean 0.6799, all 36 workloads PASSED.\n- Small timing cases (1024-4096 element shapes): candidate ~12.4-13.2 us vs reference ~9.6-10.4 us (speedup ~0.78-0.83). These look host-launch-bound.\n- Large timing cases: log10_-timing-3 (float16 4096x4096) candidate 42.8 us vs ref 15.8 us (0.37); log10_-timing-7 (bfloat16 large) 44.4 us vs ref 25.3 us (0.57); log10_-timing-11 (float32 4096x4096) 77.3 us vs ref 25.8 us (0.33).\n- Current kernel: one program per 16384 elements (2-byte dtypes) or 8192 elements (4-byte), single tl.load of the whole tile, then tl.log(x)*0.4342944819032518 staged in fp32, then a single tl.store of the whole tile. In-place (same pointer for load and store).\n\nPlease answer these specific questions with backend-native evidence for the large shapes (timing-3, timing-7, timing-11):\n1. What is the actual device kernel time vs the full call time (host launch overhead) for the large cases? I need to know how much of the 42.8/44.4/77.3 us is device work.\n2. For the big kernel, what are the MTE2 (GM->UB load), MTE3 (UB->GM store) active ratios and the Vector/AIV active ratio? Is the log instruction the bottleneck, or is the kernel serialized between load/compute/store (i.e. no overlap / no double buffering)?\n3. Is there evidence of redundant traffic, bank conflicts, or UB overflow / spilling in the compiled kernel for the 8192-element fp32 tile and the 16384-element 2-byte tile?\n4. Which of these is the most likely root cause of the ~2.4-3x gap to the reference on the large shapes, and what single experiment should the Coder run next? Competing candidates I want discriminated: (a) missing MTE2/MTE3 overlap because one monolithic tile per program cannot be software-pipelined -> use an inner loop over chunks with num_stages>1; (b) the log lowering itself being several times slower than a native log instruction -> try tl.log2-based math; (c) tile too large / UB pressure -> smaller tiles and more programs; (d) simple GM bandwidth saturation.\n\nAlso note whether the small cases show any device-side cost at all.\n\nReturn one actionable next experiment with the concrete kernel-structure change and expected effect."
      }
    }
  ],
